Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Flux Balance Analysis with Mathematica",
  FontFamily->"Comic Sans MS",
  FontSize->14]], "Input",
  Evaluatable->False,
  TextAlignment->Center,
  TextJustification->0],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Flux Balance Analysis with Mathematica",
  FontFamily->"Comic Sans MS"]], "Input",
  Evaluatable->False],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Flux Balance Analysis with Mathematica",
  FontFamily->"Comic Sans MS",
  FontSize->10]], "Input",
  Evaluatable->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["You are chief technical officer of a new biomedical \
research company, LF enterprizes, with a new blockbuster drug, code named X, \
on your hands - a cure for cancer and a Viagra substitute all in one. You \
serendipitously discoverd that the drug can be easily manufactured in E. coli \
by splicing in a gene that is highly expressed in the pituitary gland of the \
Bonobo. This is represented by the rection Rax in the diagram below. During a \
test run, the yield of X was quite good, but you need it make it even better, \
since your chief financial officer just informed you of her plans to do a \
leveraged buy out of Pfizer.\n\nOne thing you can try is to increase the \
yield of X by adjusting the glucose and oxygen flux rates on the bioreactor, \
which have the symbols O and G in the diagram. Your options range from 0 to \
60M/kgDW-hr for O, and from 0 to 40M/KgDW-hr for G. By inspecting the diagram \
below, do you think its possible to cut costs without sacrificing yield by \
giving less than the maximum of glucose and/or oxygen? Might too much of \
either actually reduce production of X? Dont worry if you can't right now - \
that's what Flux Balance Analysis is for!\n\nR stands for gRowth, and we \
assume that E. coli is willing and able to optimize the flux(es) going into \
R. The willing part means that we have succeeded in represneting E. coli's \
best interests as the node R in this model of metabolic fluxes. The able part \
means that E. coli has sufficent regulatory control over the set fluxes in \
this model to find the model optimum for R.  We can calculate the model \
optimum using the mass balance equations for each reaction, plus additional \
constraints that reflect the fact that metabolites must be conserved over all \
of the reactions. These intra and inter-reaction conservation equations are \
all linear. The optimization function, simply the magnitude of Rar, is also \
linear, thus we can use the Mathematica LinearProgramming function, that is, \
if you have Mathemaica version 4.2. If you dont, bug your TF or figure out \
how to optimize the equations using the Excel Solver, which is both powerful \
and end-user friendly.",
  FontFamily->"Comic Sans MS",
  FontSize->9]], "Input",
  Evaluatable->False],

