Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Its convenient to use a spreadsheet to  set up the \
equations for the flux model.  The workbook Flux.xls contains the matrix of \
reactions and metabolites shown below, along with some custom worksheet \
functions that create matrix definitions that are needed by the Mathematica \
LinearProgramming function. The matrix represented by the light grey box of \
cells below is called the Stoichiometric matrix. The reaction mass balance \
equations are represented by the columns, and the metabolite balance \
equations are in the rows. The rel, or relation column below can have the \
values (-1, 0, 1), which is what the LinearProgramming function accepts as \
codes for (<, =, >). Notice that the thresh colum contains 0 for all of the \
rows of the Stoichiometric matrix - this is saying that metabolites must be \
conserved across all reactions. The rows with headers C_G and C_O are the \
constraint equations for Glucose and Oxygen, and on the last column is our \
objective function.",
  FontFamily->"Comic Sans MS",
  FontSize->9]], "Input",
  FormatType->TextForm],

